Subject: [PATCH 09488/11362] The substrate under the error lifecycle:
 unwritten means forgotten

Operator directive 2026-08-21, same session as the six-step cycle: "any idea in
your head not written down will eventually be forgotten."

Recorded as a subsection of The Error Lifecycle rather than a separate rule,
because it is what the cycle rests on. Steps 3, 5 and 6 fail silently without
it: you cannot own a mistake nobody recorded, cannot correct a record never
written, cannot convert a lesson that no longer exists. An unwritten insight
does not degrade gracefully -- it is present, then absent, with no signal
between.

Why it binds harder on agents than on people: context is compacted mid-task,
sessions end without warning, several agents work one checkout with no shared
memory, and a sibling cannot ask what you were thinking. A human forgets slowly
and can reconstruct; an agent's context vanishes and reconstructs nothing.

It names the tell, because observing it prompted the directive: "worth noting"
and "worth flagging" mark the moment you decide something matters and then do
not write it down. Those appeared repeatedly in this session and the ideas
behind them went nowhere until asked for.

A routing table maps kinds of knowledge to durable homes -- rule, memory, commit
message, ops/status.md, artifact, research note -- so "write it down" has an
answer rather than being an exhortation.

Closing argument is cost asymmetry: writing costs a minute, re-deriving costs a
session, re-deriving WRONG costs more. This project already has a MANDATORY rule
that exists solely because an ARC framing kept being rediscovered and then
contradicted across sessions.

